  "eventMetadata": {
    "context_description": "Recent U.S. economic data and Federal Reserve communications have shaped trader views on the September 15-16 FOMC meeting. Inflation remains above the 2% target, with PCE readings near 4% earlier in the year and ongoing pressures from energy and supply disruptions linked to geopolitical events, prompting several officials to favor tighter policy. The July FOMC minutes, released August 19, revealed three dissents for an immediate hike and broad agreement that further tightening would likely be needed absent improvement in price data. However, the July employment report showed softening payrolls and an unemployment rate at 4.1%, easing some concerns about labor-driven inflation. These mixed signals have led markets to price a majority probability on holding the federal funds rate steady in the 3.50%-3.75% range while leaving room for a 25 basis point increase if incoming figures sustain hawkish concerns.",
    "context_requires_regen": true,
